<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talal of Jordan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Talal bin Abdullah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Talal of Jordan (Talal bin Abdullah) was King of Jordan from July 1951, after the assassination of his father Abdullah I, until August 1952. During his short reign he approved the constitution of 1952, which made the government responsible to parliament. Parliament deposed him on grounds of mental illness in favour of his son Hussein, and he spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talal-of-jordan.jpg" alt="Talal of Jordan" width="217" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jordan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talal_of_Jordan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Talal was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> on 26 February 1909, the eldest son of Abdullah bin Hussein and Musbah bint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>. Abdullah, a son of Hussein bin Ali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Transjordan in 1921. Talal was educated privately in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, graduated from the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, in 1929 and was commissioned in the cavalry of the Arab Legion. He became aide to his grandfather, the ousted King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and by 1948 he was a general. In November 1934 he married Zein al-Sharaf bint Jamil, and their son Hussein was born in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Transjordan became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordan">Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</a> after it sought independence in 1946, and Talal became crown prince. On 20 July 1951 Abdullah I was assassinated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by a Palestinian, amid rumours that he planned a peace treaty with Israel. Prince Hussein, aged fifteen and at his grandfather's side, survived. Talal became king.</p><p>His main achievement was the constitution ratified on 1 January 1952, which made the government collectively, and the ministers individually, responsible to the Jordanian Parliament. He is also judged to have eased relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Saudi Arabia. Historians differ on his attitude to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A British resident called him deeply anti-British at heart, while Avi Shlaim argues that his resentment of British interference was genuine. A set of stamps issued for his accession was withdrawn and burned after he left the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1952 the British resident, Queen Zein al-Sharaf and Prime Minister Tawfik Abu al-Huda reported that Talal was mentally ill, and he was flown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for treatment. Abu al-Huda summoned both houses of parliament, which on 11 August 1952 unanimously deposed him on medical grounds, citing schizophrenia. Abu al-Huda governed until Hussein came of age on 2 May 1953. Talal had wished to live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> but was sent to a sanatorium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where he died on 7 July 1972.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Talal of Jordan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abdullah I was assassinated in Jerusalem in 1951; after the brief reign of <b>Talal</b>, Hussein ruled from 1953 to 1999.</blockquote><a href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Talal of Jordan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
